
Start();
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking;
search GroupFields Utilities list;

register(Utilities);
define
    mlBag; mlNum;
    mlField code={
        mlNum = 7;
        cerr "ML-1 CONTROL  +/ a FIELD attaches":;
        mlBag +/ mlNum;
        mlLen := mlBag.listLengtH;
        cerr "ML-1 length = " *mlLen " (want 1)":;
        };
    mlLiteral code={
        cerr "ML-2 SUBJECT  +/ a LITERAL must refuse":;
        mlBag +/ 7;
        cerr "ML-2 DID NOT REFUSE -- a literal was attached as a member":;
        };
    mlAfter code={
        mlLen := mlBag.listLengtH;
        cerr "ML-3 length AFTER the refusal = " *mlLen " (want 1 -- nothing was attached)":;
        };
    ;
mlField(mlBag);
mlLiteral(mlBag);
mlAfter(mlBag);
cerr "MEMBERLIT SENTINEL":;
stop();

memberLitT -- +/ REFUSES A LITERAL RIGHT SIDE, BY PRESENCE.

Membership attaches a NODE to a list. A literal is a value with no node to attach, so
`bag +/ 7` is asking the membership channel to do the value channel's job. The test is
`isLiteral`, which the parse already knows -- presence, not kind. A classifier-based gate
was the withdrawn S3, and a gate that waits for one is a gate that never lands.

ML-1 IS THE ANTI-VACUITY CONTROL and is not decoration: the same operator with a FIELD on
the right must still attach, or ML-2's refusal is equally consistent with +/ being broken.
ML-3 reads the length in a LATER action, because a refusal is terminal for the action and
the line after the refused statement does not run -- so the only way to see that nothing
was attached is from outside.

